\description{
Builds the three elevation models from a point cloud, whatever its source
(spec 059):
\itemize{
\item \code{"lidar_ign"}: IGN LiDAR HD tiles delivered without their derived MNT /
MNS / MNH rasters;
\item \code{"lidar_drone"}: drone LiDAR, usually unclassified;
\item \code{"photogrammetrie"}: a structure-from-motion cloud from an RGB drone. It
only sees the canopy surface, so the terrain comes from \code{mnt_externe}.
}

The \strong{DTM} (MNT) is the ground surface, the \strong{DSM} (MNS) the highest
surface (canopy, buildings) and the \strong{CHM} (MNH) the height above ground.
}
\details{
\strong{LiDAR sources} (\code{"lidar_ign"}, \code{"lidar_drone"}) run one
\href{https://r-lidar.github.io/lasR/}{lasR} pass:
\enumerate{
\item With \code{classifier = TRUE} (default), every class is reset, isolated
points are flagged as noise (\code{classify_with_ivf()}, class 18) and the
ground is classified again (\code{classify_with_csf()}, or
\code{classify_with_ptd()}). IGN classes are thus not trusted as delivered.
\item A TIN of ground and water points (classes 2 and 9) gives the DTM.
\item The DSM is the highest non-noise point of each cell.
\item Points are normalised against the TIN and the CHM is the highest
normalised point, which is more accurate on slopes than DSM - DTM.
Negative heights are set to 0.
}

\strong{Photogrammetry} keeps the drone DSM (noise removed with
\code{classify_with_sor()}) and never derives a terrain from it. \code{mnt_externe},
typically the IGN LiDAR HD DTM, is resampled onto the DSM grid (bilinear).
A SfM cloud is often shifted vertically (no ground control points,
ellipsoidal heights): with \code{recalage_vertical = TRUE}, the shift is the
median of DSM - DTM over bare-ground cells, i.e. cells where \code{mnh_reference}
(the IGN CHM) is below 0.5 m, and it is removed from the DSM. The CHM is
DSM - DTM, negative heights set to 0.

On the IGN tile \code{LHD_FXX_0633_6767} (20.7 M points), the reclassified DTM
matches the published IGN DTM within 1.1 cm (median, 95th percentile
7.7 cm) and the CHM within 0 m (median); a 2.30 m shift added to the DSM is
recovered within 3 mm.

\strong{Outputs} are written as \code{mnt.tif}, \code{mns.tif} and \code{mnh.tif} in three
sub-directories of \code{dossier}: \verb{ign_mnt/}, \verb{ign_mns/}, \verb{ign_mnh/} for
\code{"lidar_ign"}, \verb{drone_mnt/}, \verb{drone_mns/}, \verb{drone_mnh/} otherwise. In a
project (\code{dossier} = \verb{<project>/cache/layers}), \code{\link[=resolve_project_dem]{resolve_project_dem()}} and
\code{\link[=resolve_project_chm]{resolve_project_chm()}} pick the drone products first, then the published
IGN rasters, then the \verb{ign_*} ones, and the drone products raise the
project to NDP 2. The LiDAR pass is cached: it is re-run only when the
tiles, the resolution or the classification settings change.
}
